\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev3bcom1)}{The results of this study have profound practical and social implications for the future of artificial intelligence in education. Beyond its high predictive accuracy, gTransformer contributes to the social dimension of educational AI by placing evidence-based diagnostics at the core of the learning experience. By grounding its latent context vectors in theoretical parameters—such as learning rates and prior knowledge—the model formally acknowledges that students are dynamic learners with unique interaction contexts, rather than static categorisations based on fixed capacity traits. This shift is crucial for promoting trust and transparency in AI systems, as educators can clearly understand the pedagogical rationale behind the model's differing estimations for students with identical performance records.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev3bcom1)}{From a practical perspective, our findings demonstrate how grounded deep learning models can be integrated into Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) to facilitate proactive, non-Markovian support strategies. The differentiated diagnostics provided by gTransformer allow for more nuanced instructional interventions: while a high-momentum learner might be recommended accelerated pacing to maintain engagement, a student whose profile indicates a need for foundational remediation can be identified earlier, even before significant errors occur. Consequently, gTransformer serves as a tool for proactive pedagogical support, ensuring that educational technology acts as an enabler of inclusive and tailored instruction in increasingly diverse digital classrooms.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{The representational grounding methodology explored in this work is fundamentally domain-independent, which suggests that the resulting pedagogical insights and the accuracy--interpretability trade-offs could be generalised to diverse educational contexts.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{Nonetheless, extending the empirical validation of \textit {gTransformer} beyond the benchmark datasets utilised in this study to a wider range of domains and subjects represents a valuable research direction to further illustrate its versatility.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{In this initial validation of gTransformer, the grounding process was restricted to the two primary knowledge parameters: initial mastery $P(L_0)$ and learning rate $P(T)$.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{The Guess and Slip parameters were set to fixed, population-level values estimated by the reference BKT model to prioritise the interpretability of `knowledge' acquisition over the modelling of `performance' errors.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{In established BKT literature, $P(L_0)$ and $P(T)$ are considered the primary parameters reflecting a student's latent state, whereas Guess and Slip are often viewed as performance parameters tied to the assessment item properties}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{By fixing the latter, the model remains focused on the core pedagogical constructs whilst avoiding model degeneracy challenges}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{that arise when all four parameters vary simultaneously.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{Future research could address the impact of incorporating student-specific Guess and Slip values to further refine the granularity of context-aware individualisation.}{26}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{generalisable}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{While gTransformer was validated using BKT as the primary reference model, the underlying Representational Grounding approach is inherently extensible. Within the educational domain, future research could investigate the integration of alternative theoretical frameworks, such as Additive Factor Models or Performance Factor Analysis, allowing for the use of diverse pedagogical parameters while leveraging the historical learning context-awareness of the Transformer architecture. Furthermore, the applicability of the gTransformer approach extends beyond Knowledge Tracing. The Representational Grounding framework can be adapted to any scientific field where established theoretical models coexist with high-volume empirical data. By anchoring latent representations to domain-specific concepts, this methodology offers a \replaced [id=rev]{generalisable}{generalizable} path toward bridging deep learning expressiveness with rigorous scientific grounding. Evaluating how different theoretical assumptions influence both the accuracy--interpretability trade-off and the context-awareness of domain-specific predictions across diverse disciplines remains an open and promising avenue for future research.}{27}
